WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:02.480
Narrator: Whether or not
you were ready for it,

00:00:02.480 --> 00:00:05.023
Taylor Swift is finally
back with new music.

00:00:06.080 --> 00:00:08.730
At midnight on Friday, April 26,

00:00:08.730 --> 00:00:10.800
Taylor released the presumed lead single

00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:14.150
for her seventh album and
an accompanying music video.

00:00:14.150 --> 00:00:17.270
The song, titled "Me!,"
is an energetic anthem

00:00:17.270 --> 00:00:20.390
featuring Panic! at the
Disco's Brendon Urie.

00:00:20.390 --> 00:00:22.960
Here are all the hidden
details, references,

00:00:22.960 --> 00:00:24.550
and meanings you may have missed

00:00:24.550 --> 00:00:26.392
in Taylor's "Me!" music video.

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:32.520
The video opens with a slithering snake,

00:00:32.520 --> 00:00:34.210
but it's not the darker-toned snakes

00:00:34.210 --> 00:00:35.980
of the "Reputation" era.

00:00:35.980 --> 00:00:38.680
Instead, this snake is pink and white.

00:00:38.680 --> 00:00:41.673
Suddenly, it bursts into
a cloud of butterflies.

00:00:42.540 --> 00:00:44.960
It may appear obvious, but
there's a lot of meaning

00:00:44.960 --> 00:00:46.893
packed into this one transformation.

00:00:47.780 --> 00:00:49.340
The snake is slithering on what looks

00:00:49.340 --> 00:00:51.200
like chalk drawn on pavement,

00:00:51.200 --> 00:00:53.180
which could be referring
to how Taylor wrote

00:00:53.180 --> 00:00:54.030
that she,

00:00:58.326 --> 00:01:01.510
in her "Reputation"-era
poem, "Why She Disappeared."

00:01:01.510 --> 00:01:03.200
The imagery also brings to mind

00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:05.100
the ending of that same poem:

00:01:10.230 --> 00:01:12.520
The snake, of course,
represents both Taylor

00:01:12.520 --> 00:01:14.540
and her past "Reputation" era,

00:01:14.540 --> 00:01:17.690
which we now see as
coming to an official end.

00:01:17.690 --> 00:01:19.710
The butterflies here symbolize rebirth

00:01:19.710 --> 00:01:22.530
and have been featured
heavily in the song's promo.

00:01:22.530 --> 00:01:26.350
They're also returned to what
feels like Taylor's true self.

00:01:26.350 --> 00:01:28.330
After all, butterflies were featured

00:01:28.330 --> 00:01:30.840
on Taylor's very first album cover.

00:01:30.840 --> 00:01:33.600
Lastly, the sequence
seems to directly refer

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:37.030
to lyrics from Taylor's
"Reputation"-era song "End Game,"

00:01:37.030 --> 00:01:37.925
where she sings,

00:01:40.010 --> 00:01:41.180
This line ended up being

00:01:41.180 --> 00:01:43.600
a pretty literal description of the order

00:01:43.600 --> 00:01:46.283
in which her album and
single were released.

00:01:50.691 --> 00:01:53.560
We see Taylor and Brendon
Urie in a pretty apartment,

00:01:53.560 --> 00:01:54.940
arguing in French.

00:01:54.940 --> 00:01:57.990
This is Taylor's first time
collaborating with Brendon Urie,

00:01:57.990 --> 00:02:00.310
though she's been a longtime fan of his.

00:02:00.310 --> 00:02:03.540
In her February essay on
pop music for Elle UK,

00:02:03.540 --> 00:02:06.530
Taylor may have hinted at
the pair's upcoming collab,

00:02:06.530 --> 00:02:09.260
directly naming "I Write
Sins Not Tragedies"

00:02:09.260 --> 00:02:12.330
as a special song to her at age 16.

00:02:12.330 --> 00:02:14.400
In the video, Brendon
appears to be playing

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:16.700
the role of her love interest.

00:02:16.700 --> 00:02:20.260
Behind Taylor, you can see
a sparkling Christmas tree.

00:02:20.260 --> 00:02:22.180
This is a nod to her childhood spent

00:02:22.180 --> 00:02:24.460
growing up on a Christmas tree farm.

00:02:24.460 --> 00:02:26.260
You can also spot the bar cart teased

00:02:26.260 --> 00:02:28.100
on her Instagram countdown.

00:02:28.100 --> 00:02:30.170
We see Taylor's cats Olivia and Meredith

00:02:30.170 --> 00:02:31.700
make a quick appearance,

00:02:31.700 --> 00:02:33.410
one of the other many video teasers

00:02:33.410 --> 00:02:35.380
she posted on Instagram.

00:02:35.380 --> 00:02:37.650
Taylor's first dress
resembles the one she wore

00:02:37.650 --> 00:02:40.300
while performing "Treacherous"
throughout "The Red Tour"

00:02:40.300 --> 00:02:42.300
but with flowers added.

00:02:42.300 --> 00:02:44.440
They look like the same
flowers that decorated her

00:02:44.440 --> 00:02:46.100
"Why She Disappeared" poem

00:02:46.100 --> 00:02:48.330
and her "Reputation" merch calendar,

00:02:48.330 --> 00:02:50.680
which we'll get to later.

00:02:50.680 --> 00:02:52.590
Taylor's style here also looks similar

00:02:52.590 --> 00:02:55.660
to her iconic "Blank Space" music video.

00:02:55.660 --> 00:02:58.270
Her mascara-teared, golf-club-wielding

00:02:58.270 --> 00:03:00.970
"Blank Space" persona
is given a further nod,

00:03:00.970 --> 00:03:02.967
as Taylor screams in French,

00:03:05.660 --> 00:03:07.400
Narrator: As Taylor exits the apartment,

00:03:07.400 --> 00:03:08.710
the song begins.

00:03:08.710 --> 00:03:10.750
"Me!"'s opening line sounds just like

00:03:10.750 --> 00:03:12.240
the opening of "Delicate,"

00:03:12.240 --> 00:03:15.150
the result of both using a vocoder effect.

00:03:15.150 --> 00:03:16.520
The opening lyrics call back

00:03:16.520 --> 00:03:18.830
to several classic Taylor songs.

00:03:18.830 --> 00:03:20.161
First, she sings,

00:03:23.200 --> 00:03:24.050
echoing the lyrics

00:03:24.050 --> 00:03:27.569
of her song "Jump and Fall"
and a line from "Shake it Off."

00:03:29.920 --> 00:03:31.530
We're able to spot another image

00:03:31.530 --> 00:03:33.681
Taylor teased on Instagram as she sings,

00:03:36.210 --> 00:03:38.350
Framed photos of chicks wearing sunglasses

00:03:38.350 --> 00:03:41.320
surround a portrait of the Dixie Chicks.

00:03:41.320 --> 00:03:43.120
This could be hinting at a future collab

00:03:43.120 --> 00:03:44.490
with the country group.

00:03:44.490 --> 00:03:47.100
The Dixie Chicks also tweeted
a pair of eyes at Taylor

00:03:47.100 --> 00:03:49.823
after the song dropped,
adding fuel to the fire.

00:03:53.210 --> 00:03:54.620
As the video continues,

00:03:54.620 --> 00:03:56.660
the grand staircase and towering arches

00:03:56.660 --> 00:03:59.110
Taylor glides through
resemble the throne room

00:03:59.110 --> 00:04:01.430
from the "Look What You
Made Me Do" music video,

00:04:01.430 --> 00:04:04.560
showing the contrast between
her past and present self.

00:04:04.560 --> 00:04:05.649
Taylor sings that,

00:04:08.320 --> 00:04:10.030
Likely referencing her monologue

00:04:10.030 --> 00:04:13.022
in "Look What You Made Me
Do" when she announces:

00:04:21.687 --> 00:04:23.550
We see Taylor touch a pink phone,

00:04:23.550 --> 00:04:26.480
one that only her oldest
fans may recognize.

00:04:26.480 --> 00:04:28.810
The old-fashioned phone
looks nearly identical

00:04:28.810 --> 00:04:31.190
to the one used in
Taylor's "Our Song" video

00:04:31.190 --> 00:04:33.440
from back in 2007.

00:04:33.440 --> 00:04:35.050
The color scheme in "Our Song" seems

00:04:35.050 --> 00:04:37.680
to be making a comeback in "Me!"

00:04:37.680 --> 00:04:39.290
She follows the line with:

00:04:42.020 --> 00:04:44.230
Referencing an iconic "Reputation"-era

00:04:44.230 --> 00:04:45.593
lyric from "End Game."

00:04:48.310 --> 00:04:50.120
Snake-shaped clouds float her way

00:04:50.120 --> 00:04:53.273
and try to attack her, but she
walks through them unscathed.

00:04:55.860 --> 00:04:57.360
After walking out the door,

00:04:57.360 --> 00:04:59.790
Taylor is dancing in a pastel yellow suit

00:04:59.790 --> 00:05:02.530
and joined by more and more dancers.

00:05:02.530 --> 00:05:05.700
We can spot two clues from
Taylor's Instagram countdown:

00:05:05.700 --> 00:05:09.090
the colorful tiled
pavement and briefcases.

00:05:09.090 --> 00:05:10.650
The yellow-and-white-striped sidewalks

00:05:10.650 --> 00:05:12.890
are also reminiscent of
the Yellow Brick Road

00:05:12.890 --> 00:05:14.860
from "The Wizard of Oz."

00:05:14.860 --> 00:05:17.790
"Me!" seems to be a tribute
to both Taylor's career

00:05:17.790 --> 00:05:19.523
and classic childhood movies.

00:05:22.270 --> 00:05:25.120
There's another clear movie
reference to "Mary Poppins,"

00:05:25.120 --> 00:05:27.010
as Brendon Urie jumps out of a window

00:05:27.010 --> 00:05:29.300
and floats down with an umbrella.

00:05:29.300 --> 00:05:31.540
We get a view of a glass-like sky,

00:05:31.540 --> 00:05:35.030
suggesting this video is
taking place in its own bubble.

00:05:35.030 --> 00:05:37.050
In her "Ready For It?" music video,

00:05:37.050 --> 00:05:40.760
we saw a robot Taylor doppelgänger
trapped in a glass box

00:05:40.760 --> 00:05:42.240
trying to get out.

00:05:42.240 --> 00:05:44.148
But in "Me!," it's clear Taylor's

00:05:44.148 --> 00:05:46.710
in control of her own world.

00:05:46.710 --> 00:05:48.930
We get a glimpse of Brendon's new suit.

00:05:48.930 --> 00:05:51.640
It's the same flower print
as Taylor's first dress,

00:05:51.640 --> 00:05:53.363
the poem, and the calendar.

00:05:54.260 --> 00:05:57.750
In the distance, a pink
neon sign saying "Lover"

00:05:57.750 --> 00:05:59.880
stands out against the rooftops.

00:05:59.880 --> 00:06:01.750
Fans think "Lover" could be the title

00:06:01.750 --> 00:06:04.983
of Taylor's next single
or another track on TS7.

00:06:06.390 --> 00:06:09.260
We get yet another
past-music-video reference

00:06:09.260 --> 00:06:11.230
when we see Taylor sitting on what appears

00:06:11.230 --> 00:06:12.870
to be a rooftop in Paris.

00:06:12.870 --> 00:06:15.890
The scene looks just like
one from Taylor's music video

00:06:15.890 --> 00:06:17.190
for "Begin Again."

00:06:17.190 --> 00:06:19.630
Her reference to "Begin
Again" also looks like

00:06:19.630 --> 00:06:22.060
it relates to the selective all-caps words

00:06:22.060 --> 00:06:24.120
she dropped in her Instagram captions

00:06:24.120 --> 00:06:25.840
starting back in October.

00:06:25.840 --> 00:06:30.247
Together, the all-caps words
read, "Begin Another Me."

00:06:31.310 --> 00:06:33.970
Brendon is joined by
umbrella dancers on the roof,

00:06:33.970 --> 00:06:35.740
which are a reference to the dancers

00:06:35.740 --> 00:06:38.700
who performed with Taylor
during "How You Get the Girl"

00:06:38.700 --> 00:06:41.290
on her "1989 World Tour."

00:06:41.290 --> 00:06:44.440
Brendon offers Taylor
flowers, which she refuses,

00:06:44.440 --> 00:06:46.500
and then offers her an engagement ring,

00:06:46.500 --> 00:06:48.020
which she also turns down.

00:06:48.020 --> 00:06:49.383
She only accepts a kitten.

00:06:50.940 --> 00:06:53.310
Fans took a lot away from this exchange.

00:06:53.310 --> 00:06:55.450
Before the premiere, Taylor told fans

00:06:55.450 --> 00:06:57.260
that there was a surprise
she'd been hiding

00:06:57.260 --> 00:06:59.340
for months in this video.

00:06:59.340 --> 00:07:01.440
Fans were convinced this
meant she was engaged

00:07:01.440 --> 00:07:03.870
to her boyfriend, Joe Alwyn.

00:07:03.870 --> 00:07:06.410
However, Taylor rejects the proposal,

00:07:06.410 --> 00:07:08.420
similar to the empty engagement ring box

00:07:08.420 --> 00:07:09.253
that caused commotion

00:07:09.253 --> 00:07:10.086
in her "Look What You Made Me Do" video.

00:07:11.550 --> 00:07:15.420
Instead, the only way you get
this girl is with a kitten.

00:07:15.420 --> 00:07:17.090
This left fans thinking that perhaps

00:07:17.090 --> 00:07:19.600
the surprise announcement
was a new kitten.

00:07:19.600 --> 00:07:22.200
This theory would add up
with the three cats present

00:07:22.200 --> 00:07:23.350
in the mural she commissioned

00:07:23.350 --> 00:07:25.470
in Nashville to promote the song.

00:07:25.470 --> 00:07:27.860
Taylor then confirmed the
theory the following morning

00:07:27.860 --> 00:07:30.320
with a post about her newest kitten.

00:07:30.320 --> 00:07:33.170
There are some other small
Easter eggs in this scene.

00:07:33.170 --> 00:07:35.060
We see both the heart-shaped earrings

00:07:35.060 --> 00:07:37.990
and the pink ruffled dress
Taylor teased on Instagram

00:07:37.990 --> 00:07:39.360
before the release.

00:07:39.360 --> 00:07:40.940
We transition into the next scene

00:07:40.940 --> 00:07:42.950
through what looks like a kaleidoscope

00:07:42.950 --> 00:07:45.010
of loud heartbeats under coats,

00:07:45.010 --> 00:07:47.200
bringing to life lyrics from Taylor's song

00:07:47.200 --> 00:07:48.610
"Welcome to New York."

00:07:48.610 --> 00:07:50.190
We find Taylor and Brendon dancing

00:07:50.190 --> 00:07:52.090
to a crowd of cheering fans.

00:07:52.090 --> 00:07:54.670
Taylor's wearing a
'60s-inspired heart dress,

00:07:54.670 --> 00:07:57.320
another countdown clue from Instagram.

00:07:57.320 --> 00:07:59.500
The scene also draws contrast to one

00:07:59.500 --> 00:08:01.000
in "Look What You Made Me Do,"

00:08:01.000 --> 00:08:03.010
where we saw Taylor on a stage in front of

00:08:03.010 --> 00:08:05.410
a sea of robot-model "friends."

00:08:05.410 --> 00:08:08.713
In "Me!," the fake friends are
replaced with adoring fans.

00:08:11.220 --> 00:08:13.810
Taylor next appears in
a marching-band outfit,

00:08:13.810 --> 00:08:16.930
which is a callback to her
"You Belong With Me" video.

00:08:16.930 --> 00:08:19.540
The outfit is also another Instagram clue.

00:08:19.540 --> 00:08:21.730
The dancers then lift her
up into the same position

00:08:21.730 --> 00:08:24.250
she was held in during
"I Did Something Bad"

00:08:24.250 --> 00:08:25.780
on the "Reputation" tour.

00:08:25.780 --> 00:08:27.690
As she and Brendon run to what looks like

00:08:27.690 --> 00:08:30.000
a mansion melded from townhouses,

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:32.960
we can view iconic London
structures in the distance.

00:08:32.960 --> 00:08:37.870
There's Big Ben, St Paul's
Cathedral, and Tower Bridge.

00:08:37.870 --> 00:08:41.190
The structures could be a
nod to Joe's British roots

00:08:41.190 --> 00:08:43.983
and that Taylor herself may
be moving across the pond.

00:08:44.870 --> 00:08:46.830
It's been reported that
Taylor is interested

00:08:46.830 --> 00:08:48.490
in a permanent home in London,

00:08:48.490 --> 00:08:50.750
so this could be her way of telling fans

00:08:50.750 --> 00:08:52.690
that her move is in progress.

00:08:52.690 --> 00:08:55.830
Taylor and Brendon dance
together on a rainbow platform.

00:08:55.830 --> 00:08:58.170
Taylor's wearing white cowboy boots,

00:08:58.170 --> 00:09:00.440
perhaps a nod to her country roots.

00:09:00.440 --> 00:09:02.140
Their cheeky dance is reminiscent

00:09:02.140 --> 00:09:04.140
of the dance from "The Parent Trap,"

00:09:04.140 --> 00:09:05.613
another classic movie.

00:09:08.490 --> 00:09:10.410
The video's final scene shows Taylor

00:09:10.410 --> 00:09:14.692
and Brendon dancing around as
colorful rain splatters down.

00:09:14.692 --> 00:09:16.100
This could be a reference to

00:09:16.100 --> 00:09:17.670
Taylor's "Delicate" music video

00:09:17.670 --> 00:09:20.560
where she dances in the street
in the middle of the rain.

00:09:20.560 --> 00:09:24.090
In "Delicate," a song widely
believed to be about Joe,

00:09:24.090 --> 00:09:24.967
she sings:

00:09:31.230 --> 00:09:33.250
In "Me!" we see Taylor's lover

00:09:33.250 --> 00:09:36.040
joyously dance around
in the mess with her.

00:09:36.040 --> 00:09:38.170
The tips of her hair are colored blue.

00:09:38.170 --> 00:09:39.840
The look is similar to the pink tips

00:09:39.840 --> 00:09:42.120
Taylor rocked in "I Knew You Were Trouble"

00:09:42.120 --> 00:09:43.780
and the pink hair she's been sporting

00:09:43.780 --> 00:09:45.430
to promote this new era.

00:09:45.430 --> 00:09:48.520
In the background, we can spot
the last Instagram teaser,

00:09:48.520 --> 00:09:50.100
a blue bike.

00:09:50.100 --> 00:09:51.840
As the song comes to an end,

00:09:51.840 --> 00:09:54.550
Taylor gives us one last extra detail

00:09:54.550 --> 00:09:56.890
that only she would create.

00:09:56.890 --> 00:09:59.770
The song is 3 minutes and 13 seconds long,

00:09:59.770 --> 00:10:02.420
while the video is 4
minutes and 9 seconds.

00:10:02.420 --> 00:10:05.170
Four plus 9 equals, yeah.

00:10:05.170 --> 00:10:06.990
Taylor's lucky number.

00:10:06.990 --> 00:10:10.240
The video for "Me!" is a
triumphant tribute to Taylor

00:10:10.240 --> 00:10:13.460
full of subtle references to
past songs and their videos.

00:10:13.460 --> 00:10:16.170
She gives us color, nostalgia,

00:10:16.170 --> 00:10:18.150
and perhaps most significantly,

00:10:18.150 --> 00:10:20.330
a celebration of self.

00:10:20.330 --> 00:10:22.110
While Taylor's previous era showed her

00:10:22.110 --> 00:10:25.340
literally killing and
burying her past selves,

00:10:25.340 --> 00:10:28.190
this new era begins by celebrating them.

00:10:28.190 --> 00:10:29.091
She sings,

00:10:30.980 --> 00:10:33.140
Accepting that all Taylors past,

00:10:33.140 --> 00:10:38.140
present, and future are just
really the one, true Taylor.

00:10:38.420 --> 00:10:40.970
Fans first started speculating
that Taylor had new music

00:10:40.970 --> 00:10:44.170
to release as far back as October 2018

00:10:44.170 --> 00:10:46.850
when she posted a photo of
herself playing Scrabble

00:10:46.850 --> 00:10:49.507
with the caption, "Let the games BEGIN."

00:10:50.440 --> 00:10:52.810
Swifties were quick to notice
that Taylor's Scrabble tiles

00:10:52.810 --> 00:10:55.010
added up to seven points,

00:10:55.010 --> 00:10:57.800
taking the number as a clue towards TS7.

00:10:57.800 --> 00:11:00.970
Anticipation for a new
album resurfaced in February

00:11:00.970 --> 00:11:05.070
with another Instagram post,
this time of seven palm trees.

00:11:05.070 --> 00:11:07.830
Fans got their biggest clue on April 13

00:11:07.830 --> 00:11:10.170
when Taylor posted a photo
of a heart-shaped earring

00:11:10.170 --> 00:11:12.660
with the caption, "4.26"

00:11:12.660 --> 00:11:14.870
as well as updated her official website

00:11:14.870 --> 00:11:17.600
with a mysterious countdown set to end on,

00:11:17.600 --> 00:11:20.080
you guessed it, 4/26.

00:11:20.080 --> 00:11:21.800
Fans lost it when they realized

00:11:21.800 --> 00:11:23.850
that April 13 just so happened

00:11:23.850 --> 00:11:25.690
to be National Scrabble Day,

00:11:25.690 --> 00:11:29.110
tying back to their original
theories and seeming to confirm

00:11:29.110 --> 00:11:31.950
that the countdown would end with TS7.

00:11:31.950 --> 00:11:34.870
Even more, the day
April 13 was also marked

00:11:34.870 --> 00:11:37.440
on Taylor's official
"Reputation"-era calendar

00:11:37.440 --> 00:11:39.550
released back in July 2018.

00:11:39.550 --> 00:11:41.680
This signaled to fans
that her new music release

00:11:41.680 --> 00:11:44.470
had been planned for quite some time.

00:11:44.470 --> 00:11:46.840
Taylor followed her initial 4.26 post

00:11:46.840 --> 00:11:49.040
with a series of
aesthetically similar photos

00:11:49.040 --> 00:11:50.680
with the same caption,

00:11:50.680 --> 00:11:52.450
leading fans to believe the images were

00:11:52.450 --> 00:11:54.680
a sneak peek to the video we finally got

00:11:54.680 --> 00:11:56.670
as the countdown came to an end.

00:11:56.670 --> 00:11:58.490
The next marked dates on Tay's calendar

00:11:58.490 --> 00:12:01.340
are 5/13 and 7/13.

00:12:01.340 --> 00:12:03.230
Could these be hinting at future single-

00:12:03.230 --> 00:12:05.560
or even album-release dates?

00:12:05.560 --> 00:12:06.763
Only time will tell.

00:12:07.620 --> 00:12:09.190
Did you notice any other details

00:12:09.190 --> 00:12:10.630
from Taylor's "Me!" release?

00:12:10.630 --> 00:12:12.980
Make sure to let us know
in the comments below.

